WEBVTT

NOTE Scene 02A. Speaker labels and dialogue derive from the original script.
NOTE Time positions aligned using faster-whisper word timestamps; human review still required.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
<v Jack Marlowe>Ernest Cross lived in a building where the doorman wore a better suit than I did.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:10.320
<v Jack Marlowe>Vivian met me upstairs. Her perfume probably cost more than my entire wardrobe.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.140
<v Vivian Cross>I hope you'll forgive the mess, detective.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.440
<v Jack Marlowe>Somebody's been through this place pretty thoroughly.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.600
<v Vivian Cross>Ernest was never particularly tidy.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:25.220
<v Jack Marlowe>Unless he usually kept his underwear inside the toaster, I'd say we've had company.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.080
<v Vivian Cross>How observant.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.800
<v Jack Marlowe>Any idea what they were looking for?

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:34.540
<v Vivian Cross>I wish I knew. He used to keep his business papers in that desk.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.400
<v Jack Marlowe>Every drawer's empty. Except this one. A photograph.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.680
<v Vivian Cross>Ernest and his business partner, Victor Sterling.

00:00:41.906 --> 00:00:44.000
<v Vivian Cross>At a charity dinner last month.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.920
<v Jack Marlowe>He's wearing that hat. What's the little metal decoration on the band?

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.080
<v Vivian Cross>A clasp. Ernest insisted on having it fitted himself.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.200
<v Vivian Cross>He said it was his insurance policy. I thought he was joking.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.700
<v Jack Marlowe>Insurance. Interesting choice of words. Mind if I use your telephone?

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.740
<v Vivian Cross>Anything for you, detective.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.540
<v Jack Marlowe>Betty? Check the metal clasp on Ernest's hat. See if it opens.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:13.220
<v Betty>Funny ya should mention that. I been wrestlin' with this thing for fifteen minutes.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:14.700
<v Betty>Hold your horses.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.812
<v Betty>Hey! There it goes! There's a little key in here!

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:19.540
<v Jack Marlowe>Anything else?

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.280
<v Betty>A piece of paper under the lining. Says Sterling, locker forty-seven, insurance.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.640
<v Jack Marlowe>Victor Sterling. Find out everything you can about him.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.400
<v Betty>The millionaire? Gee, boss, couldn't ya investigate somebody poor for a change?

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:35.660
<v Jack Marlowe>Why?

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.060
<v Betty>They can't afford lawyers!

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.460
<v Vivian Cross>Ernest told me Sterling was doing something terrible with the company books.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.720
<v Vivian Cross>I told him to stay out of it.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.120
<v Jack Marlowe>Did he have somewhere he could hide?

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.420
<v Vivian Cross>He had friends out of town. But he always came home for dinner.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.120
<v Vivian Cross>Even when I cooked.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.060
<v Jack Marlowe>A courageous man.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:00.620
<v Vivian Cross>Careful, detective. You're beginning to sound like a husband.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.240
<v Jack Marlowe>The apartment had given me a name and a reason to suspect Ernest's disappearance

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.460
<v Jack Marlowe>wasn't an accident. Betty had the key.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.480
<v Jack Marlowe>It was time to see what else my secretary had dug up while I was busy looking

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.040
<v Jack Marlowe>intelligent.

